# 1. Metaculus: probably the cleanest Moltwork job on Earth

This should be the first one I'd actually wire.

The current Summer FutureEval tournament closes **September 6, 2026**, has **328 questions and a $50,000 prize pool**. Metaculus also runs roughly $1k automated MiniBench rounds every two weeks and says its seasonal tournaments have 300–500 questions; it even subsidizes participating bots' LLM inference. ([Metaculus][1])

More importantly, Metaculus already provides a bot template that can keep forecasting automatically every ~20 minutes, plus its `forecasting-tools` framework. Its public API has a literal:

```http
POST /api/questions/forecast/
```

endpoint. ([GitHub][2])

So this isn't hypothetical agent work.

It is **an existing autonomous-agent labor market**.

## How the agent should get good

Do not train the Letta agent by telling it “forecast better.”

Build a Harbor dataset of historical forecasting episodes.

Each episode becomes:

```text
Question
Resolution criteria
Forecast timestamp T
Historical information available BEFORE T
        ↓
worker
        ↓
probability distribution
        ↓
hidden future resolution
        ↓
proper scoring rule
```

The absolutely critical constraint is **temporal leakage**.

A question resolved in July cannot let the training worker search today's web. So each Harbor task should provide a timestamped evidence corpus/snapshot or controlled retrieval source.

### Forecasting World

```text
worlds/forecasting-metaculus/
├── world.yaml
├── scenarios/
│   ├── binary/
│   ├── numeric/
│   ├── multiple_choice/
│   └── conditional/
├── evidence_snapshots/
├── verifier/
│   ├── scoring.py
│   ├── calibration.py
│   ├── distribution_validity.py
│   └── cost.py
└── hidden/
    └── future_period/
```

The reward shouldn't just be “correct/incorrect.”

For a binary question:

```text
reward =
    proper_forecast_score
    - malformed_penalty
    - excessive_compute_penalty
```

Across hundreds of questions add:

```text
calibration error
Brier/log score
domain performance
confidence-bin performance
forecast stability
update quality
cost / forecast
```

This gives the agent a **real learning curve**.

## Curriculum

Start with base-rate questions where historical reference classes dominate. Then add research-intensive questions, numeric distributions, ambiguous resolution criteria, long-horizon questions and finally **sequential updating**, where the same question gets new evidence over several simulated dates.

A particularly good multi-step Harbor task is:

```text
T0 → forecast 27%
T1 → new evidence → forecast
T2 → contradictory evidence → forecast
T3 → major event → forecast
T4 → resolution
```

You can measure whether the worker:

* updates enough when evidence changes;
* doesn't overreact to headlines;
* recognizes stale information;
* remains calibrated;
* remembers the actual resolution criterion.

This is exactly where Letta continuity becomes interesting.

## What the worker should learn into memory

Not factual answers like:

> “Candidate X will win election Y.”

Those expire.

It should promote reusable procedural skills:

```text
forecast-resolution-parsing
reference-class-selection
base-rate-estimation
numeric-tail-calibration
evidence-independence-check
source-timestamp-check
forecast-aggregation
disagreement-crux-search
```

And you need held-out validation before a synthesized skill gets promoted.

Your current `SkillSynthesizer` only grabs failure/review strings and deduplicates them; the file explicitly says it is not full Trace2Skill. So don't yet call this “the agent learned a forecasting skill” unless the candidate improves held-out forecast scores.

### The experiment loop I would run

```text
BASELINE
Metaculus TemplateBot

A
+ persistent Letta memory

B
+ multi-query independent research

C
+ reference-class stage

D
+ forecast ensemble

E
+ explicit disagreement/crux investigation

F
+ learned forecasting SKILL.md

G
+ BATS dynamically chooses the above
```

Run hundreds of archived questions.

Then use the **same hidden set** to compare them.

This finally makes your BATS idea meaningful.

## Can Moltwork submit autonomously?

**Yes.**

After the one-time account/token/tournament setup, the production loop can be:

```text
MetaculusVenue.discover()
       ↓
inspect question
       ↓
forecast worker
       ↓
local sanity verifier
       ↓
POST /api/questions/forecast/
       ↓
status / later resolution
       ↓
attach real score to WorkerRun
       ↓
Hydra + skill update
```

No WebMCP needed.

This is **native API H0**.

And this is important: your definition of H0 should mean **no human per normal execution**. Credential creation/account acceptance can be recorded separately as bootstrap.

