and archetype-specific/opponent-belief policies. ([Kaggle][4])

That's extremely useful evidence for how we should structure YGO.

## Kill the fake YGO World

You already have the replacement staring at you.

`sbl1996/ygo-agent` has:

```text
ygoenv
    high-performance Yu-Gi-Oh environment
    envpool + ygopro-core
    standard Gym interface

ygoai
    RL agents
```

with ambitions around self-play, deckbuilding, fast adaptation to new decks and cards, MCTS and league methods. ([GitHub][5])

So I would completely replace the deterministic YGO environment presently used in `mw`.

Not extend it.

Replace it.

## The Harbor/YGO architecture

There are actually two agents.

### Scientist worker

This is your Letta/Moltwork agent.

It can use expensive LLMs, source code, experiments, statistics, Git, memory, etc.

Its job is to **develop the game-playing policy**.

### Match policy

This is the submitted/evaluated program.

It must operate under the simulator's latency/network/runtime restrictions.

So:

```text
             LETTA SCIENTIST
                    │
           proposes experiment
                    │
                    ▼
              Git branch
                    │
       ┌────────────┴─────────────┐
       │                          │
   policy code                deck config
       │                          │
       └────────────┬─────────────┘
                    ▼
              YGO Harbor World
                    │
             10,000 matches
                    │
        ┌───────────┼────────────┐
        ▼           ▼            ▼
     baseline    population   holdout-meta
        │           │            │
        └───────────┴────────────┘
                    ▼
                 score
                    │
             experiment result
                    │
                  Hydra
                    │
             Letta reflection
```

That's much better than letting an LLM “play Yu-Gi-Oh.”

## Make micro-worlds

Full matches give sparse reward.

Harbor should also generate **saved game-state puzzles**.

For example:

```text
world: ygo.lethal_detection
world: ygo.interruption_timing
world: ygo.resource_preservation
world: ygo.combo_selection
world: ygo.go_first
world: ygo.go_second
world: ygo.side_deck
world: ygo.opponent_archetype_identification
```

Take states from real self-play trajectories and ask:

> Which legal action maximizes expected match win probability?

That gives dense, cheap training.

Then the full-match World verifies whether those improvements transfer.

## League training matters

Don't optimize against one opponent.

Maintain something like:

```text
population/
├── frozen_baseline_v1
├── heuristic_control
├── historical_best_001
├── historical_best_002
├── aggressive_policy
├── control_policy
├── current_meta_A
└── current_meta_B
```

Then score:

```text
0.45  weighted meta win rate
0.20  frozen-baseline win rate
0.15  held-out deck robustness
0.10  exploitability / worst matchup
0.05  runtime
0.05  legality / crashes
```

Otherwise the scientist will simply discover exploits against its latest training opponent.

That would give you a **real evolutionary laboratory**.

## Deckbuilding should be a separate genome

Don't merge everything into one blob.

```text
PolicyGenome
DeckGenome
SideDeckGenome
ExperimentGenome
```

Then ask BATS:

```text
Is expected value higher from:
 - improving policy?
 - switching archetype?
 - changing three cards?
 - opponent-model routing?
 - adding search?
```

This gets fascinating when you ingest live tournament results.

Eventually the Oracle can say:

> “New banlist produced a meta shift. Current YGO worker has high transfer score on combo decks but weak performance into control. Expected value of retraining deck family X is highest.”

That's the sort of thing Moltwork should become.

## Autonomous submission?

Pokémon's **Simulation** track is over: final submissions locked on **August 16, 2026**, with games continuing afterward. The Strategy track remains open until September 13, but participation required entering the Simulation track; its entry deadline was August 9. ([Kaggle][3])

For future Kaggle simulation competitions, submission itself is highly automatable—the artifact is just the tournament-defined bundle and Kaggle supports CLI/MCP workflows around competition assets and replays. ([Kaggle][6])

But Yu-Gi-Oh itself currently lacks a comparable official **bot-for-cash API venue**.

So classify it:

```text
YGO TRAINING     H0
YGO EVALUATION   H0
YGO DECK RESEARCH H0
REAL HUMAN YCS   H3/H4
FUTURE BOT COMP  potentially H0
```

Don't lie to Oracle and describe Yu-Gi-Oh prize tournaments as autonomous agent work.

Instead describe YGO as:

```text
