# WebMCP: important distinction

This is worth encoding correctly in Oracle.

WebMCP does **not** mean:

> “Any website can now be operated reliably by an agent.”

It means:

> “A website can explicitly expose structured tools to browser agents.”

It's still in Chrome's origin-trial/experimental phase. Sites themselves register imperative tools or annotate forms declaratively. ([Chrome for Developers][13])

Therefore Oracle's rule should be:

```text
site has HTML form
    != WebMCP

browser agent can click it
    != WebMCP

document.modelContext exposes tools
    = WebMCP
```

Your discovery browser should actually inspect the page for WebMCP.

Then store:

```yaml
action_surface:
  type: webmcp
  discovered_tools:
    - submit_entry
    - get_status
  mutation: true
  evidence_url: ...
  checked_at: ...
```

This turns WebMCP adoption itself into an Oracle signal.

And it fits the WebMCP work you've already specced in the repo.

